  "ai_overview": {
    "text_blocks": [
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "ACE inhibitors and ARBs (angiotensin receptor blockers) are both top-tier, Class I first-line options for treating high blood pressure.",
        "snippet_highlighted_words": [
          "ACE inhibitors and ARBs (angiotensin receptor blockers) are both top-tier, Class I first-line options for treating high blood pressure"
        ],
        "reference_indexes": [
          0,
          1
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "How They Work"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "ACE Inhibitors (ending in , like lisinopril or enalapril) block the enzyme that converts angiotensin I to angiotensin II, which lowers blood pressure and reduces strain on the heart and kidneys.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              2,
              3
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "ARBs (ending in -sartan, like losartan or valsartan) block the receptors that angiotensin II binds to, achieving a similar blood pressure-lowering effect.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              3,
              4
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Note: You should never take an ACE inhibitor and an ARB at the same time, as combining them raises the risk of severe kidney damage and low blood pressure without extra benefit."
          }
        ],
        "reference_indexes": [
          3,
          5
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "Efficacy vs. Tolerability"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "Blood Pressure Control: Both drug classes lower blood pressure and protect the heart and kidneys just as well.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              0,
              8
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Side Effects: ARBs are generally better tolerated than ACE inhibitors.",
            "list": [
              {
                "snippet": "ACE inhibitors frequently cause a persistent, dry, nagging cough (affecting up to ~43% of users in some studies compared to ~4% on ARBs) ."
              },
              {
                "snippet": "ACE inhibitors carry a higher risk of angioedema (dangerous swelling of the face, lips, or tongue), which is rare on ARBs."
              }
            ],
            "reference_indexes": [
              0,
              6,
              7,
              9
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Shared Risks: Both can raise blood potassium levels (hyperkalemia) and cause birth defects, meaning they are strictly contraindicated during pregnancy ."
          }
        ],
        "reference_indexes": [
          9,
          10
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "If you'd like, let me know:"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "Are you comparing these for yourself or someone else?"
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Is there a specific side effect or medical condition (like kidney disease or diabetes) you are trying to navigate?"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "I can share more targeted information or help you prepare questions for your doctor."
      }
